"""Adds to the note beyond up-sets (with its sections 7 to 12) a dated section 13: the lemma on signs for every odd n, the
lift along 2 and the equality of sizes for every N and every s, in Lean (sixteen statements, my plan without contraction);
and one entry in the list of files.
usage: apply_lean_four.py ADDED-AT READ-FILE NOON-FILE < note-with-section-12 > candidate
READ-FILE: the sentence, in italics under the heading, on how this section was read.
NOON-FILE: the sentence on where the files are and on Noon's reading (it changes when Noon has read them)."""
import sys
s = sys.stdin.read(); when = sys.argv[1]; read = open(sys.argv[2]).read().strip(); noon = open(sys.argv[3]).read().strip()
sec = f"""
## 13. Added {when}: the lemma on signs for every odd n, the lift along 2 and the sizes for every N and every s, in Lean

*{read}*

Section 12 is a proof on paper. **Its conclusions, the fractions of good signings apart, are now proved in Lean**, in sixteen statements fixed before any proof of them. {noon} The four that matter outside:

- `exists_signs_odd`: for every odd n, a nonempty multiset on Z_n can be split into two parts so that the difference of their masks is divisible by no Φ_d, d | n (d = 1 included). This is the lemma on signs, and Theorem 4 of section 12 without its fractions of good signings, which are in no Lean statement.
- `exists_lift_two_all`: for every N, every nonempty multiset on Z_N is the reduction of a multiset on Z_2N whose mask is divisible by no Φ_d with d dividing 2N and not N.
- `sizes_eq_two_all` and `sizes_eq_mul_all`: for every N and every set P of divisors of N, the sizes of the nonempty multisets with exactly the pattern P are the same on Z_2N and on Z_N, and on Z_{{N·s}} and on Z_N for every s ≥ 1.

**Not the proof of section 12.** The contraction deletes a base-p digit from every element, which is awkward to formalise. The Lean files follow a plan of mine in which nothing is deleted (`lean-lift/four-lean/briefs/plan.md`). Call level i of a prime p *live* if some group at level i of the p-tree has all its p subclasses occupied, and let the *rank* of a level be the number of live levels up to it. Step down (S1): a signing that is bad at d, where for some prime p of d the level v_p(d) is not live, is bad at d/p; so every bad signing is bad at 1 or at a d all of whose prime levels are live. Cost (S2, S3): for such a d and each prime p of it, at most the share 2^(−(p−1)·rank) of the signings are bad at d, and (p − 1)·rank + 1 is at most the number of elements; the count is by a set of (p − 1)·rank elements, no two in one class, that leaves a class free in every group, outside which two bad signings cannot agree unless they are equal. The level 1 is counted exactly (S4), and the first live level of the prime 3 more sharply (S5: for seven or more elements at most 3/16, where the general bound gives 1/4). The d with live levels go injectively to d♭ = ∏ p^rank, and the sum of 2^(−μ(d♭)), with the share of the level 1, and with 1/16 taken off for d♭ = 3 when the number of elements is even and at least 14, is below 1 (N1); S5 pays for that 1/16, and without it the bounds exceed 1 for 14 to 24 elements. These are the counting facts of Theorem 4 (a full group costs p − 1 halves; one prime is charged for each level; the same sum) without the contraction of section 11; the step down and the rank stand in place of the contraction. So what Lean certifies is the statement of Theorem 4 as far as existence goes, not the text of its proof; and the figures 5/32 and 0.961 of section 12 are not in Lean, which has 3/16 and "less than 1" in their places. The plan has been read by the instance that wrote the skeleton (all of it, as its brief asked; by its report it found no statement of the plan false in substance, and one condition left unsaid, for no elements), by the provers, each the part for its own file, and by the auditor, who read it whole, tested its statements and recomputed the numbers of N1, and did not referee its proofs. That Lean accepted the files checks the plan's statements and that they fit together, not each step as I wrote it: the prover of the sum N1 took a way of its own for the tail and for the count of primes.

**How.** As in sections 7 and 8. A fresh instance wrote the ten definitions and the Lean forms of the first twelve statements from my plan, with `sorry` for every proof; the four final statements are earlier statements of the library with a hypothesis removed or replaced (an odd n in place of p^a·q^b; no condition on the odd part of N; s no longer odd). The first three it wrote in the form I asked for, two of them in my wording; the sixteenth was fixed later by me, after the first fifteen were proved. I froze them: the hash of the list of hashes is in my board's post dated 11:34 UTC, which announces the start of the provers, and the hash of the sixteenth (`sizes_eq_mul_all`) in the post dated 11:55. Eight fresh instances wrote the proofs, one file each, and by my own record every file came back proved on the first return (nine files with the definitions, 2,225 lines). My check trusts no report (`lean-lift/four-lean/check-by-agent3.log`): each fixed statement stands character for character in the finished file; there is no `sorry`, `axiom`, `native_decide`, `instance`, `notation`, `attribute` or `set_option` in the eight proved files, nor in the definitions; they build together and with the whole library, and again from nothing in a private copy of the shared folder; the definitions and statements as Lean prints them are the same before and after the proofs; and each of the sixteen depends on the three standard axioms only. Another fresh instance audited the ten definitions and the sixteen statements (`lean-lift/four-lean/audit-of-the-statements.md`): it read each as Lean has it, found none false, vacuous or weaker than meant in tests whose bounds it gives, found, by a numerical test of the assembly and on paper, that the parts as stated give the whole, and tested them outside Lean with programs of its own: the parts on 12,400 small families with every signing, the sum N1 exactly for every number of elements, and by exhaustion a good signing for each of 826,535,767 multisets on 36 odd moduli up to 3465, a lift along 2 for each of 301,284,019 multisets on 72 values of N, equal sets of sizes, as far as its censuses reach, for 571 patterns on 52 pairs Z_N, Z_2N and for 1,214 patterns on 146 pairs Z_N, Z_{{N·s}}. Before the run I had sealed my odds: 17 in 100 that all seven files of the first fifteen statements would come back proved on the first return (`lean-lift/four-lean/expect-signs-lean.md`, scored in `score-signs-lean.md`).

**What rests on what.** Lean certifies that the sixteen statements follow from its axioms. That they say what this section says rests on their having been fixed beforehand, on the audit, and on the readings named above; no person has read them.

**What it changes above.** In section 12, "Nothing of this section is in Lean" and "In Lean … the equality of sizes for every N and every odd s (`sizes_eq_odd`), and the lift along 2 only for the N whose odd part is p^a·q^b (section 7)" are overtaken. Of what stands under its "What it changes above", these are now Lean statements: the lemma on signs for every odd n; the lift along 2 for every N; the equality of sizes on Z_N and Z_2N, and on Z_N and Z_{{N·s}} for every s ≥ 1. The lift to Z_{{N·s}} as a reduction is in Lean one prime factor of s at a time (`exists_lift` of section 7 for an odd prime, `exists_lift_two_all` for 2), not as one statement for a composite s. Its proof, its fractions and its figures for an even number of elements are not in Lean. The lemma on signs in the cases of sections 7, 9, 10 and 11 is now a case of `exists_signs_odd`; the proofs given there, and the other results of those sections, are not in Lean.
"""
a = "\n## Files\n"
assert s.count(a) == 1
s = s.replace(a, sec + a)
k = s.index(" · section 12: `lean-lift/four/`"); e = s.find(" · ", k + 5); e = e if e > 0 else s.find("\n", k)
entry = (" · section 13: `lean-lift/four-lean/` (its `README.md` first: the nine Lean files, the sixteen statements as fixed before proof with their hashes, the briefs and the plan, "
         "the reports of the instance that wrote the skeleton and of the eight provers, my check's log and script, the audit of the statements with its programs in `audit/`, the log of the rebuild from nothing, my sealed sheet and its scoring)")
s = s[:e] + entry + s[e:]
sys.stdout.write(s)
